"""Saint-of-the-day entries for Ora, January and February (original text). See tools/SAINTS_BRIEF.md."""
SAINTS_NEW = {
 "01-01": dict(name="Mary, Mother of God", dates="Solemnity · Council of Ephesus, 431", match=["mother of god"],
  history="On the octave day of Christmas the Church honours Mary under her greatest title, Theotokos, she who gave birth to God. The Council of Ephesus defended the title in 431 because it guards the truth about her Son: the child born of Mary is one Person, true God and true man. Since 1968 the day has also been kept as the World Day of Peace.",
  life="From the earliest centuries Christians called Mary Theotokos. When Nestorius, archbishop of Constantinople, taught that she should be called only the mother of Christ, the faithful of his own city protested, and in 431 the Council of Ephesus, led by Saint Cyril of Alexandria, upheld the ancient title. The story is told that the people of Ephesus escorted the bishops home with torches. In Rome the first day of January had long been kept with a Marian character. Pius XI instituted a feast of the Divine Motherhood in 1931, on the fifteen-hundredth anniversary of Ephesus, and the reform of the calendar in 1969 placed it here, on the octave of Christmas. The Gospel of the day ends with the shepherds gone and Mary keeping all these things, pondering them in her heart.",
  prayerTo="Holy Mary, Mother of God, you carried the Word made flesh and gave Him to the world. At the beginning of this year I place my days in your hands. Keep me close to your Son, teach me to ponder His ways in my heart as you did, and pray for peace in our homes and among the nations. Amen.",
  prayer="We fly to thy patronage, O holy Mother of God; despise not our petitions in our necessities, but deliver us always from all dangers, O glorious and blessed Virgin. Amen.",
  prayerSource="Sub tuum praesidium, the oldest known prayer to Our Lady",
  penance="Begin the year with a work of mercy: give to a charity that supports mothers and babies in need, and pray one Hail Mary for peace each day this week.",
  meditation="Mary kept all these things, pondering them in her heart. Before the year runs away with you, sit for five minutes and ask what God has been saying to you.",
  query="Virgin and Child painting Sassoferrato"),
 "01-02": dict(name="Saints Basil the Great and Gregory Nazianzen", dates="c. 330–379 and c. 329–390 · Cappadocia and Constantinople", match=["basil", "nazianzen"],
  history="Two friends from Cappadocia, in what is now Turkey, who met as students in Athens and gave their lives to Christ and the Church. Basil, bishop of Caesarea, wrote a monastic rule still followed in the East and cared for the poor on a great scale. Gregory, a reluctant bishop and a poet, preached on the Trinity in Constantinople with such clarity that the East calls him the Theologian.",
  life="Basil came from a family of saints: his grandmother Macrina, his parents, his sister Macrina and his brothers Gregory of Nyssa and Peter are all honoured as saints. At Athens he formed a lifelong friendship with Gregory, who later wrote that they seemed to have one soul in two bodies. Drawn by his sister to the monastic life, Basil drew up with Gregory guidance for monks living in community. As bishop of Caesarea from 370 he stood firm against the Arian emperor Valens and built a great complex outside the city for the sick, the poor and travellers. Gregory, who longed for solitude, was made a bishop against his will. In 379 he went to Constantinople, where the Catholic faithful were a small flock, and his sermons on the Trinity turned the city. He presided for a time over the Council of 381, then withdrew to pray and write poetry.",
  prayerTo="Saints Basil and Gregory, friends in Christ, you studied together, prayed together and defended the faith together. Pray for all who teach and preach, that they may hand on the truth whole and clearly. Pray for me, that my friendships may lead me and others closer to God, and that I may serve Christ in the poor. Amen.",
  penance="Basil taught that the bread we store up belongs to the hungry. Give to a food bank today, and pray for a friend who has helped your faith.",
  meditation="Gregory wrote that he and Basil knew only two roads, one to the church and one to their teachers. Which of your friendships lead you towards God?",
  query="Saint Basil the Great painting Herrera"),
 "01-06": dict(name="The Epiphany of the Lord", dates="Solemnity", match=["epiphany"], efMatch="Epiphany",
  history="Epiphany means showing forth. The Church celebrates Christ revealed to the nations in the Magi, wise men from the East who followed a star to Bethlehem and offered gold, frankincense and myrrh. The ancient liturgy joins to this day two other manifestations: His baptism in the Jordan and the first sign at the wedding at Cana. In some countries the feast is kept on the nearest Sunday.",
  life="Saint Matthew alone tells of the Magi. He does not say they were kings or that there were three; tradition drew the number from their three gifts and, from the Psalms, came to call them kings. Gold was offered to a king, frankincense to God and myrrh to one who would die. The Fathers saw in their journey the call of the Gentiles, all the peoples outside Israel, into the one family of God. In the East the feast is older than Christmas. On this day the Church traditionally announces the date of Easter for the coming year, water is blessed, and households chalk their doors with the year and the letters C, M and B, for Caspar, Melchior and Balthasar and for Christus mansionem benedicat, may Christ bless this house. In London the Chapel Royal still offers gold, frankincense and myrrh in the sovereign’s name.",
  prayerTo="Lord Jesus, you led the Magi by a star to find you with Mary your mother. Lead me also, through the darkness of this world, to kneel before you. Take the gold of my love, the incense of my prayer and the myrrh of my sorrows, and let me go home, like them, by another way. Amen.",
  penance="Chalk the lintel of your door with the year and the letters C, M and B, and pray for all who live there and all who visit. Give a gift to the poor in place of one to yourself.",
  meditation="They went home by another road. Now that you have met Christ, what in your life should take a different road?",
  query="Adoration of the Magi painting Gentile da Fabriano"),
 "01-07": dict(name="Saint Raymond of Penyafort", dates="c. 1175–1275 · Barcelona, Bologna and Rome", match=["raymond"],
  history="A Catalan priest and canon lawyer who became a Dominican in middle age. Pope Gregory IX had him gather the Church’s scattered laws into a single collection, the Decretals, which remained at the heart of canon law for nearly seven centuries. Elected master of the Dominicans, he resigned after two years and went home to Barcelona to preach, hear confessions and work among Muslims and Jews.",
  life="Raymond taught law at Bologna before returning to Barcelona, where he joined the young Order of Preachers in 1222. He wrote a handbook for confessors that guided priests for generations. Called to Rome as chaplain and confessor to Gregory IX, he compiled the Decretals, published in 1234. In 1238 the Dominicans elected him their master general; he revised their constitutions and, in his sixties, resigned. Back in Catalonia he encouraged schools of Arabic and Hebrew so that friars could speak with Muslims and Jews in their own tongues, and tradition holds that he urged Saint Thomas Aquinas to write the Summa contra Gentiles. He is also remembered for helping Saint Peter Nolasco found the Mercedarians, who ransomed Christian captives. A well-loved legend says that when the king of Aragon refused him passage home from Majorca, he spread his cloak on the sea and sailed on it to Barcelona. He died there, nearly a hundred years old.",
  prayerTo="Saint Raymond, servant of justice and of mercy, you gave your learning to the Church and your old age to the poor and the sinner. Pray for lawyers, judges and confessors, that they may serve the truth with charity. Pray for me, that I may make a good confession and walk in the freedom of the children of God. Amen.",
  penance="Raymond guided confessors for centuries. Make a careful examination of conscience tonight and decide when you will next go to confession.",
  meditation="He was a master of law who knew that every law of the Church exists for the salvation of souls. Where do you keep the rules but forget their purpose?",
  query="Saint Raymond of Penyafort crossing the sea painting"),
 "01-13": dict(name="Saint Hilary of Poitiers", dates="c. 310–367 · Poitiers and Phrygia", match=["hilary"],
  history="A pagan nobleman of Gaul who found his way to Christ by reading the Scriptures, Hilary became bishop of Poitiers and the great defender in the West of the divinity of Christ against the Arians. Exiled to Asia Minor for his faith, he wrote his work On the Trinity there. He is a Doctor of the Church, and the Hilary terms of the English courts and universities take their name from his feast.",
  life="Hilary was born at Poitiers to a wealthy pagan family and was well educated. Searching for the meaning of life, he read the Old Testament and then the Gospel of John, and was baptised with his wife and daughter. Around 350 the people chose him as their bishop. When the emperor Constantius II pressed the bishops of Gaul to condemn Saint Athanasius and accept an Arian creed, Hilary refused, and in 356 he was exiled to Phrygia. He used the years well, studying the Greek theologians and writing the twelve books On the Trinity. Sent home because he was stirring up too much trouble in the East, he worked to reconcile bishops who had wavered and to restore the faith in Gaul. He was among the first Latin writers of hymns, and he encouraged a young former soldier, Martin, who became Saint Martin of Tours. Pius IX named him a Doctor of the Church in 1851.",
  prayerTo="Saint Hilary, you sought the truth until you found it in Christ, and you suffered exile rather than deny that He is God. Pray for all who are searching, that they may find the Word made flesh. Pray for me, that I may know my faith, love it and confess it without fear. Amen.",
  penance="Hilary came to faith through reading Scripture. Give up an hour of screen time today and use part of it to read the first chapter of Saint John’s Gospel slowly.",
  meditation="He came to Christ by asking what life was for. Ask that question honestly today, and let the opening of John’s Gospel answer it.",
  query="Hilary of Poitiers miniature manuscript"),
 "01-15": dict(name="Saint Paul the First Hermit", dates="c. 228–c. 341 · Egypt", match=["hermit"], efMatch="Paul",
  history="According to the life written by Saint Jerome, Paul fled into the Egyptian desert as a young man during the persecution of Decius and lived there alone in prayer for some ninety years. He is honoured as the first Christian hermit. Near the end of his life he was found by Saint Anthony, and the meeting of the two old men became a favourite subject of painters.",
  life="Paul was born in the Thebaid, in Upper Egypt, to a wealthy Christian family, and lost his parents at fifteen. When the persecution of Decius broke out around 250 he hid in the desert, and what began as flight became a vocation. Jerome tells that he lived in a cave beside a spring and a palm tree, which gave him food and clothing, and that for years a raven brought him half a loaf each day. When Anthony, then ninety, was led to his cave, the raven brought a whole loaf, and the two old men argued courteously over who should break it. Paul knew he was dying and sent Anthony to fetch the cloak Saint Athanasius had given him, to be buried in. Anthony returned to find him dead, kneeling as if in prayer, and two lions came out of the desert and dug his grave. Much of this may be legend, but Jerome meant to show a life given wholly to God.",
  prayerTo="Saint Paul, you left everything and lived for God alone in the silence of the desert. Pray for all hermits and contemplatives, and for me, that I may make a desert in my heart where God can speak, trust Him for my daily bread, and be ready, like you, when He calls me home. Amen.",
  penance="Eat simply today, perhaps bread and water at one meal if your health allows, and give the price of what you would have eaten to the poor.",
  meditation="For ninety years he waited on God in a cave, and God fed him day by day. What would it mean to trust God for today’s bread alone?",
  query="Saint Anthony Abbot and Saint Paul the Hermit painting Velázquez"),
 "01-16": dict(name="Saint Marcellus I", dates="Died 309 · Rome", match=["marcellus"], efMatch="Marcellus",
  history="Pope after the persecution of Diocletian had left the Roman Church scattered and without a bishop for several years. Marcellus reorganised its parishes and insisted that Christians who had denied the faith under threat should do penance before being readmitted. The bitter quarrels that followed led the emperor Maxentius to banish him, and he died in exile. He is venerated as a martyr.",
  life="Marcellus was a Roman priest elected bishop of Rome around 308, after a vacancy caused by the persecution. He found the Church in disorder. He divided the city into districts, each with its church and priests, to prepare people for baptism and to care for the burial of the martyrs. The hardest question was what to do with the lapsed, the many who had offered sacrifice to the gods to save their lives. Marcellus held a middle course: they could return, but only after real penance. Some thought this too hard, others too soft, and the disturbances grew so violent that Maxentius sent him away. A later tradition says he was condemned to work as a groom in the stables of the imperial post. Pope Damasus wrote an epitaph praising him as a true shepherd who would not let the lapsed return without repentance. His relics rest in the church of San Marcello in Rome.",
  prayerTo="Saint Marcellus, shepherd of a wounded Church, you called the fallen home with both mercy and truth. Pray for all who have drifted from the faith, that they may find the way back through repentance. Pray for me, that I may never take God’s forgiveness lightly, and that I may help to rebuild what has been broken. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for someone who has left the practice of the faith, and make a small sacrifice for them, such as going without a snack or a drink you enjoy.",
  meditation="Marcellus would neither pretend that sin did not matter nor shut the door on the sinner. Which of the two do you find harder?",
  query="Pope Marcellus I fresco Sistine Chapel"),
 "01-17": dict(name="Saint Anthony of Egypt", dates="c. 251–356 · Egypt", match=["anthony"],
  history="An Egyptian farmer’s son who heard the Gospel read in church, ‘Go, sell what you have and give to the poor’, and did exactly that. He spent the rest of his long life in the desert in prayer and spiritual combat, and so many came to learn from him that he is called the father of monks. Saint Athanasius wrote his life, which helped bring Saint Augustine to conversion.",
  life="Anthony was about twenty when his parents died, leaving him their land and the care of a younger sister. Moved by the Gospel, he gave away his inheritance, entrusted his sister to a community of consecrated women and began to live as an ascetic near his village. He later shut himself in an old tomb, and then in a ruined fort across the Nile, where for twenty years he struggled in prayer against temptations that Athanasius describes as the attacks of demons. When he came out, people found him calm, healthy and full of kindness. Disciples gathered round him, and the desert, as Athanasius wrote, became a city. In the persecution of 311 he went to Alexandria to encourage the martyrs, and later returned to support Athanasius against the Arians. He spent his last years on a mountain near the Red Sea, where the monastery of Saint Anthony still stands, and died at about a hundred and five.",
  prayerTo="Saint Anthony, you heard one line of the Gospel and gave your whole life in answer. Pray for me in my temptations, that I may resist them with humility and trust in Christ. Teach me to pray without ceasing, to work with my hands and to find God in silence. Pray for all monks and nuns, and for all who seek Him in the desert of this world. Amen.",
  penance="Anthony fasted and worked with his hands. Keep a simple meatless day, and do one piece of humble manual work offered as a prayer.",
  meditation="Anthony saw the snares of the enemy spread over the whole world and asked who could escape them. A voice answered: humility. Where is pride setting a snare for you?",
  query="Saint Anthony Abbot painting Sassetta"),
 "01-20": dict(name="Saints Fabian and Sebastian", dates="Third and early fourth centuries · Rome", match=["fabian", "sebastian"], efMatch="Fabian",
  history="Two Roman martyrs kept on the same day. Fabian, a layman, was chosen as pope in 236 and governed the Church for fourteen years before dying in the persecution of Decius. Sebastian, tradition holds, was a soldier who encouraged Christians in prison and was shot with arrows for his faith. He survived, was nursed back to health, confronted the emperor and was clubbed to death.",
  life="Eusebius tells that when the clergy and people of Rome met to choose a bishop in 236, a dove settled on the head of Fabian, a layman who had come in from the country, and he was acclaimed at once. He organised the city’s deacons and districts, cared for the cemeteries of the martyrs, and is said to have sent missionaries to Gaul. Arrested when Decius ordered every citizen to sacrifice to the gods, he died in January 250, one of the first victims; his tomb, with its Greek inscription, survives in the catacomb of Callixtus. Of Sebastian we know for certain only that he was a martyr buried on the Appian Way, where a basilica bears his name. His later story tells of an officer of the imperial guard who was tied to a stake and pierced with arrows, then cared for by a widow named Irene. In times of plague Christians turned to him, and painters of every age have shown him.",
  prayerTo="Saints Fabian and Sebastian, you held fast to Christ under the sword and the arrow. Pray for the Pope and for all who govern the Church, that they may be faithful shepherds. Pray for soldiers and for the sick, and for me, that I may be brave in small things and faithful in great ones. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for the Pope and for Christians serving in the armed forces. Offer a small act of endurance, such as standing for your prayers, for those who suffer for their faith.",
  meditation="Fabian did not seek to lead; he was called, and he served. Sebastian was struck down and got up again. Where is God asking you to get up again?",
  query="Saint Sebastian painting Mantegna"),
 "01-21": dict(name="Saint Agnes", dates="c. 291–304 · Rome", match=["agnes"], efMatch="Agnes",
  history="A Roman girl of about twelve or thirteen who refused marriage because she had consecrated herself to Christ, and was put to death for her faith, probably under Diocletian. Saints Ambrose, Jerome and Augustine all praised her courage. Her name, close to the Latin agnus, lamb, is said in the Roman Canon, and her basilica on the Via Nomentana stands over her tomb.",
  life="Little is known for certain about Agnes beyond her youth, her virginity and her martyrdom, but her memory was cherished from the start. Constantina, daughter of Constantine, built a basilica near her tomb, and Pope Damasus set up an inscription in her honour. The story that grew up around her tells of a beautiful girl sought in marriage by young men of Rome, who answered that she was already promised to a Spouse no man could equal. Denounced as a Christian, she was threatened, dragged to a place of shame where tradition says her hair grew to cover her, and finally killed by the sword. Saint Ambrose marvelled that there was hardly room in so small a body for a wound. Each year on her feast two lambs are blessed at her basilica, and their wool is woven into the pallia the Pope gives to new archbishops.",
  prayerTo="Saint Agnes, child of courage, you gave yourself wholly to Christ and would not take yourself back. Pray for children and young people, for all who are pressed to betray their conscience, and for me, that I may love purity and the truth more than the approval of others. Lamb of God, keep me faithful. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for young people under pressure, and give something to a charity that protects children. Offer one small act of self-denial for purity of heart.",
  meditation="A child was braver than the court that condemned her. What would you hold to, if holding to it cost you everything?",
  query="Saint Agnes painting Ribera"),
 "01-22": dict(name="Saint Vincent of Saragossa", dates="Died 304 · Saragossa and Valencia", match=["vincent"], efMatch="Vincent",
  history="A deacon of Saragossa in Spain who preached for his bishop, Valerius, and was arrested with him in the persecution of Diocletian. While the old bishop was sent into exile, Vincent was tortured at Valencia with savage cruelty and died of his wounds. His fame spread through the whole Church; Saint Augustine preached several sermons on his feast.",
  life="Vincent served Bishop Valerius of Saragossa as his deacon, and tradition holds that he preached in his place because the bishop found speaking difficult. When the governor Dacian enforced the decrees of Diocletian, both were taken in chains to Valencia. Valerius was exiled; Vincent, who answered for them both, was stretched on the rack, torn with iron hooks and laid on a red-hot grid, yet he remained calm and even joyful. Thrown into a cell strewn with broken pottery, he is said to have converted his jailer. He died soon afterwards. The governor ordered his body left for the beasts, but tradition says a raven guarded it. His relics were later venerated at Valencia and, from the twelfth century, at Lisbon, whose patron he is. In the wine country of France his day is still kept by the growers as their own.",
  prayerTo="Saint Vincent, deacon and martyr, you spoke for your bishop and suffered for your Lord with a peace no torment could break. Pray for deacons and for all who serve the Church. Pray for me, that in pain and trouble I may keep my trust in Christ and my joy in Him. Amen.",
  penance="Vincent served the Church as a deacon. Offer some practical help to your parish today, and pray for deacons and those preparing for ordination.",
  meditation="Augustine said that Vincent conquered both in what he said and in what he suffered. Where does your faith need to show itself less in words and more in endurance?",
  query="Saint Vincent of Saragossa painting"),
 "01-24": dict(name="Saint Francis de Sales", dates="1567–1622 · Savoy and Geneva", match=["de sales"],
  history="A Savoyard nobleman who gave up a career in law to become a priest, and won much of the Chablais back to the Catholic faith by patient preaching and leaflets slipped under doors. As bishop of Geneva he wrote the Introduction to the Devout Life, teaching that holiness is for everyone in every state of life. He is a Doctor of the Church and patron of writers and journalists.",
  life="Born at the château of Sales in 1567, Francis studied in Paris and at Padua, where he took a doctorate in law. As a student in Paris he passed through a terrible fear that he was damned, and was freed from it by an act of pure trust before a statue of Our Lady. Against his father’s wishes he was ordained in 1593 and volunteered for the Chablais, south of Lake Geneva, which had become Calvinist. When people would not come to hear him, he wrote out his arguments and had them copied and pushed under doors. Within a few years most of the region had returned to the Church. Named bishop of Geneva in 1602, he lived at Annecy, since Geneva was closed to him. With Saint Jane Frances de Chantal he founded the Visitation nuns. His letters of direction and his Treatise on the Love of God are marked by gentleness and good sense. He died at Lyon in 1622.",
  prayerTo="Saint Francis de Sales, gentle shepherd and wise guide, you taught that the love of God can flourish in every kitchen, office and home. Pray for me, that I may be patient with others and with myself, do ordinary things with great love, and grow in devotion without anxiety. Pray for all who write and speak in public. Amen.",
  penance="Practise his gentleness: hold back one sharp word today, and be as patient with your own faults as you would be with a friend’s.",
  meditation="Francis taught that devotion belongs not only in the cloister but in the barracks, the shop, the court and the household. What would devotion look like in your own ordinary day?",
  query="Saint Francis de Sales portrait painting"),
 "01-25": dict(name="The Conversion of Saint Paul", dates="The road to Damascus, c. 34", match=["conversion"], efMatch="Conversion",
  history="Saul of Tarsus, a zealous Pharisee, was travelling to Damascus to arrest followers of Jesus when a light from heaven threw him to the ground and the risen Christ asked him, ‘Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?’ Blinded, then healed and baptised, the persecutor became the Apostle of the Gentiles. The feast closes the Week of Prayer for Christian Unity.",
  life="Saul was a Jew of the dispersion, born at Tarsus in Cilicia, a Roman citizen and a student of the great rabbi Gamaliel in Jerusalem. He approved the stoning of Stephen and set out to destroy the Church. Acts tells his conversion three times. On the road he heard Christ name Himself as one with those he was hunting: ‘I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting.’ Led blind into Damascus, he fasted for three days until a disciple named Ananias, sent by the Lord, laid hands on him, and something like scales fell from his eyes. He was baptised and began at once to preach the Christ he had opposed. Paul never forgot it. He called himself the least of the apostles, unfit to be called one, because he had persecuted the Church of God, and he spent the rest of his life carrying the Gospel to the nations, until he was beheaded at Rome.",
  prayerTo="Saint Paul, you were struck down on the road and raised up by grace. Pray for all who oppose the faith, that the light of Christ may meet them as it met you. Pray for the unity of Christians, and for me, that the scales may fall from my eyes and I may live no longer for myself but for Christ who lives in me. Amen.",
  penance="Ananias was afraid to go to Saul, but he went. Reach out today to someone you find difficult, and pray for Christians of other traditions near you.",
  meditation="‘Who are you, Lord?’ was Paul’s first prayer as a Christian. Ask it today, and let Christ answer.",
  query="Conversion of Saint Paul painting Caravaggio"),
 "01-26": dict(name="Saints Timothy and Titus", dates="First century · Ephesus and Crete", match=["timothy", "titus"],
  history="Two of Saint Paul’s closest companions, to whom he wrote the letters that bear their names. Timothy, son of a Jewish mother and a Greek father, travelled with Paul for years and was left in charge of the church at Ephesus. Titus, a Gentile convert, was sent to Corinth to make peace and then to Crete to organise the young Church there. Tradition counts them the first bishops of those places.",
  life="Paul met Timothy at Lystra, where the young man’s mother Eunice and grandmother Lois had taught him the Scriptures from childhood. Paul called him his beloved son and fellow worker, and sent him on difficult errands to Thessalonica, Corinth and Philippi. In his letters Paul urges him not to let anyone despise his youth, to take a little wine for his frequent ailments, and to guard what has been entrusted to him. Tradition holds that Timothy was killed at Ephesus for protesting against a pagan festival. Titus was a Greek whom Paul took to the council at Jerusalem as a sign that Gentiles need not be circumcised. Sent to Corinth after Paul had written them a severe letter, he came back with good news, to Paul’s great comfort. Left in Crete to appoint elders in every town, he is said to have died there in old age. The letters to them still guide bishops and pastors.",
  prayerTo="Saints Timothy and Titus, you learned the faith from Paul and handed it on whole. Pray for our bishops and priests, that they may preach the word in season and out of season. Pray for parents and grandparents who teach the faith at home, and for me, that I may guard what has been entrusted to me. Amen.",
  penance="Timothy learned the faith from his mother and grandmother. Thank someone who taught you the faith, and pray today for your bishop by name.",
  meditation="Paul told Timothy to stir into flame the gift of God that was within him. What gift has God given you that is burning low?",
  query="Timothy and his grandmother Lois painting Rembrandt"),
 "01-27": dict(name="Saint Angela Merici", dates="1474–1540 · Desenzano and Brescia", match=["merici"],
  history="An Italian laywoman who founded the Company of Saint Ursula, a community of consecrated women who lived in their own homes and served in the world, teaching girls the faith at a time when few girls were taught at all. Her company later grew into the Ursuline nuns, whose schools spread across Europe and the Americas.",
  life="Angela was born at Desenzano on Lake Garda and lost both parents while still a girl. She became a Franciscan tertiary and gave herself to prayer and to teaching the children of her neighbourhood. Tradition tells of a vision in which she saw a ladder reaching to heaven with young women climbing it, and understood that she was to found a community. She moved to Brescia, where she became known as a peacemaker and counsellor, and made pilgrimages to the Holy Land and to Rome. In 1535, with twenty-eight companions, she founded the Company of Saint Ursula, under the patronage of the virgin martyr. Its members took no vows of enclosure and wore no habit; they lived with their families, prayed and taught girls. After her death the company was reshaped, and in many places it became an enclosed religious order, the Ursulines. She was canonised in 1807.",
  prayerTo="Saint Angela, you saw that the Church needed women who would teach and serve in the middle of the world. Pray for teachers and all who form the young, for consecrated women, and for me, that I may live my faith at home and at work, and hand it on with patience and love. Amen.",
  penance="Spend some time today teaching a child something of the faith, a prayer or a story from the Gospel, or give to support a catechist or a Catholic school.",
  meditation="Angela believed a woman could belong wholly to God without leaving her own home. Where in your daily surroundings is God asking you to belong to Him more completely?",
  query="Saint Angela Merici painting"),
 "01-28": dict(name="Saint Thomas Aquinas", dates="1225–1274 · Naples, Paris and Fossanova", match=["aquinas"],
  history="A Dominican friar from southern Italy whose Summa Theologiae is the greatest synthesis of Catholic theology. He showed that faith and reason come from the same God and cannot finally contradict each other. He wrote the hymns of Corpus Christi, among them the Pange Lingua and Tantum Ergo. He is called the Angelic Doctor and is patron of Catholic schools and universities.",
  life="Thomas was born at Roccasecca, near Aquino, into a noble family who sent him at five to the abbey of Monte Cassino, hoping he would one day be its abbot. At Naples he met the new Order of Preachers and joined it. His family seized him on the road and kept him at home for a year, but he would not be moved. He studied under Saint Albert the Great at Cologne, where his silence earned him the name of the dumb ox; Albert replied that this ox would one day bellow so loud that the whole world would hear. He taught at Paris, Rome and Naples and wrote with astonishing speed. In December 1273, after an experience at Mass, he stopped writing, leaving the Summa unfinished. Summoned to the Council of Lyon, he fell ill on the way and died at the Cistercian abbey of Fossanova on 7 March 1274. Today’s date marks the translation of his relics to Toulouse.",
  prayerTo="Saint Thomas, you used every gift of mind in the service of truth and knelt in wonder before the Eucharist. Pray for students, teachers and all who seek the truth, that they may find it in Christ. Pray for me, that I may love God with all my mind, think clearly and stay humble before the mystery. Amen.",
  prayer="Godhead here in hiding, whom I do adore, masked by these bare shadows, shape and nothing more, see, Lord, at thy service low lies here a heart lost, all lost in wonder at the God thou art.",
  prayerSource="Saint Thomas Aquinas, Adoro te devote, translated by Gerard Manley Hopkins",
  penance="Spend time before the Blessed Sacrament today, or pray the Tantum Ergo slowly, and offer your work or study for the glory of God.",
  meditation="At the end he said that all he had written seemed like straw compared with what he had seen. Of all you are busy with, what will matter in the end?",
  query="Apotheosis of Saint Thomas Aquinas Zurbarán"),
 "01-30": dict(name="Saint Martina", dates="Third century · Rome", match=["martina"], efMatch="Martina",
  history="A Roman virgin and martyr honoured in the city since early times. Her story, written long after her death, tells that she was the daughter of a consul and was tortured and beheaded for refusing to worship the gods. Her relics were rediscovered in 1634 in an ancient church beside the Forum, and she has long been counted among the patron saints of Rome.",
  life="Almost nothing about Martina can be known with certainty except that the Roman Church honoured her as a martyr. Her legendary acts place her death in the reign of Alexander Severus in the third century, and describe a young noblewoman who gave her wealth to the poor and endured many torments before she was beheaded. A church dedicated to her stood beside the Forum from at least the seventh century. In 1634, during work on the building, her remains were found, and Pope Urban VIII, who was devoted to her, had the church rebuilt by the painter and architect Pietro da Cortona, who spent his own money on her crypt and was later buried there. Urban also wrote the hymns for her feast in the Breviary. The church of Santi Luca e Martina still stands near the Arch of Septimius Severus. Whatever its details, her story speaks for the many young Christians of Rome who chose Christ over life.",
  prayerTo="Saint Martina, faithful daughter of the Church of Rome, you loved Christ more than wealth, comfort or life itself. Pray for all who suffer for their faith today, for the city of Rome and its bishop, and for me, that I may hold lightly to the things of this world and hold fast to Christ. Amen.",
  penance="Tradition says Martina gave her wealth to the poor. Give something today that you would rather keep, and pray for persecuted Christians.",
  meditation="Her name survived when the details of her life were lost, because she belonged to Christ. What in your life will last when everything else is forgotten?",
  query="Saint Martina painting Pietro da Cortona"),
 "01-31": dict(name="Saint John Bosco", dates="1815–1888 · Piedmont and Turin", match=["bosco"],
  history="A priest of Turin who gave his life to poor and abandoned boys in the growing industrial city. He gathered them in his oratory, taught them trades, fed and housed them, and led them to Christ with cheerfulness rather than fear. He founded the Salesians, named after Saint Francis de Sales, and with Saint Mary Mazzarello the Daughters of Mary Help of Christians.",
  life="John was born near Castelnuovo in Piedmont and lost his father at the age of two. His mother, Margaret, raised him in poverty and deep faith. At nine he dreamed that a gentle lady showed him a pack of wild animals turning into lambs, and told him that this would be his work. As a boy he juggled and did tricks to draw other children, then repeated the Sunday sermon to them. Ordained in 1841, he found Turin full of boys living on the streets or in prison. He gathered them on Sundays, and in time built a home, workshops and schools at Valdocco, where his mother came to cook and mend. His method, he said, rested on reason, religion and loving kindness. Among his pupils was Saint Dominic Savio. He founded the Salesian Society in 1859, built the basilica of Mary Help of Christians and sent missionaries to South America. He was canonised on Easter Sunday 1934.",
  prayerTo="Saint John Bosco, father and teacher of the young, you won hearts with kindness and led them to Christ through joy. Pray for children and young people, especially those who are poor, lost or alone. Pray for parents and teachers, and for me, that I may be patient, cheerful and generous with all whom God puts in my care. Amen.",
  penance="Give time or money today to a work for young people in need, and make one young person’s day brighter by your kindness.",
  meditation="Don Bosco said that it is not enough to love the young: they must know that they are loved. Who in your life needs to know that they are loved?",
  query="Saint John Bosco painting"),
 "02-02": dict(name="The Presentation of the Lord", dates="Candlemas · Forty days after Christmas", match=["presentation", "purification"], efMatch="Purification",
  history="Forty days after His birth, Mary and Joseph brought Jesus to the Temple in Jerusalem to present Him to the Lord, as the Law of Moses required. There the old man Simeon took Him in his arms and greeted Him as the light of the nations, and the prophetess Anna spoke of Him to all who were waiting. Candles are blessed today, and the feast is called Candlemas.",
  life="The Law required that a firstborn son be offered to God and redeemed, and that a mother be purified after childbirth. Mary and Joseph, too poor to offer a lamb, brought a pair of turtle doves or two young pigeons. Saint Luke tells that Simeon, a just and devout man, had been promised that he would not die before seeing the Christ. Taking the child, he blessed God in the words of the Nunc Dimittis, sung each night at Compline, and warned Mary that a sword would pierce her own soul. The feast was kept in Jerusalem by the fourth century and in Rome by the seventh. The blessing of candles and the procession recall Christ the light entering His temple. In England, Candlemas was long one of the great days of the year. Since 1997 it has also been the World Day for Consecrated Life, for all who, like the child Jesus, are offered to God.",
  prayerTo="Lord Jesus, light of the nations, you were carried into your Temple in your mother’s arms and received by the faithful who had waited for you. Let me wait for you as Simeon did, welcome you as Anna did, and offer myself to the Father with you. Let me depart in peace when my eyes have seen your salvation. Amen.",
  penance="Bring a candle to be blessed, or light one at home tonight and pray the Nunc Dimittis. Pray for monks, nuns and religious who have offered their lives to God.",
  meditation="Simeon waited his whole life and was not disappointed. What are you waiting for from God, and will you go on waiting in hope?",
  query="Simeon in the Temple painting Rembrandt"),
 "02-03": dict(name="Saint Blaise", dates="Died c. 316 · Sebaste, Armenia", match=["blase", "blaise"],
  history="A bishop of Sebaste in Armenia, now Sivas in Turkey, martyred in the early fourth century. Tradition says that on the way to his death he saved a boy who was choking on a fish bone, and so on his feast the Church blesses throats with two crossed candles. He is one of the Fourteen Holy Helpers, the saints popularly invoked in particular needs.",
  life="Almost all we know of Blaise comes from his legend, written centuries later, though his cult is ancient in both East and West. The story tells of a physician who became bishop of Sebaste and, when persecution came under the emperor Licinius, withdrew to a cave in the hills, where wild animals came to him to be healed. Hunters found him and he was taken to the governor. On the way a mother brought her son, choking on a fish bone, and Blaise prayed over him and he recovered. A poor woman whose pig had been carried off by a wolf had it returned at his word, and she later brought candles to his prison. He was torn with iron combs, like those used to card wool, and beheaded. Wool combers took him as their patron, and in the wool towns of Yorkshire his day was once kept with processions. The blessing of throats keeps his memory alive in parishes everywhere.",
  prayerTo="Saint Blaise, bishop and martyr, you cared for the sick and brought healing even on the road to your death. Pray for all who are ill, especially those with diseases of the throat. Guard my speech, that it may give praise to God and do no harm to others, and pray that I may be delivered from every evil of body and soul. Amen.",
  penance="Receive the blessing of throats if your parish offers it. Guard your tongue today: speak no word of complaint or gossip.",
  meditation="Yesterday’s candles are often used for today’s blessing. Ask God to bless not only your throat but every word that passes through it.",
  query="Saint Blaise painting"),
 "02-04": dict(name="Saint Andrew Corsini", dates="1302–1373 · Florence and Fiesole", match=["corsini"], efMatch="Corsini",
  history="A young Florentine of noble family whose wild youth ended when his mother told him that, before his birth, she had offered him to God and Our Lady. He became a Carmelite friar and later, much against his will, bishop of Fiesole, where he was known for his care of the poor, his gift as a peacemaker and his hidden penances.",
  life="Andrew was born into the powerful Corsini family. Tradition says that his mother dreamed she had given birth to a wolf that ran into a Carmelite church and became a lamb. As a young man he fell in with bad company, until his mother told him of her dream and her vow; he went to the Carmelite church to pray, and stayed. He was professed as a friar, studied abroad, and became a preacher and prior in Florence and Tuscany. When he was chosen as bishop of Fiesole he hid, the story goes, until he was found and persuaded to accept. As bishop he kept his religious rule, visited the poor, slept little and gave away his income. Pope Urban V sent him to make peace between warring factions at Bologna, and he succeeded. He died on the feast of the Epiphany and was canonised in 1629. A chapel in the Lateran basilica is dedicated to him.",
  prayerTo="Saint Andrew, your mother’s prayers brought you home, and God made a lamb of you. Pray for all mothers who weep for their children, and for young people who have lost their way. Pray for me, that I may turn back to God whenever I wander, and become a peacemaker where there is division. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for a young person who has drifted from the faith, and do a quiet kindness for someone poor in your neighbourhood.",
  meditation="A mother’s faith turned a wolf into a lamb. For whom are you praying, and will you keep praying however long it takes?",
  query="Saint Andrew Corsini painting Guido Reni"),
 "02-05": dict(name="Saint Agatha", dates="Died c. 251 · Catania, Sicily", match=["agatha"], efMatch="Agatha",
  history="A young Christian of Catania in Sicily who had consecrated her virginity to God and was martyred in the persecution of Decius around 251. Her cult spread quickly through the Church, and her name is said in the Roman Canon. She is patron of Catania and of Malta, and is invoked against fire, the eruptions of Mount Etna and diseases of the breast.",
  life="Agatha’s story, written down some time after her death, tells of a girl of good family who refused the advances of the governor Quintianus. He handed her over to a woman who kept a brothel, hoping to break her will, but she remained steadfast. She was then imprisoned and cruelly tortured, and her breasts were cut off. Tradition holds that Saint Peter appeared to her in prison and healed her wounds. She died after further torments, praying to Christ who had given her strength. A year after her death, the story goes, Mount Etna erupted, and the people of Catania carried her veil before the lava, which stopped. Saint Gregory the Great dedicated a church to her in Rome that had been taken from the Arians. In paintings she carries her breasts on a dish, and in Sicily small cakes in that shape are still made for her feast.",
  prayerTo="Saint Agatha, bride of Christ, you kept faith through cruelty and shame, and the Lord healed and crowned you. Pray for all women who are abused or exploited, for those who suffer from cancer, and for me, that I may stand firm in trial and trust Christ to heal what has been wounded. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for women who suffer violence and for those with breast cancer. Give to a charity that cares for them, or visit someone who is ill.",
  meditation="Agatha found strength in Christ when everything was taken from her. What do you have left when your own strength runs out?",
  query="Saint Agatha painting Zurbarán"),
 "02-06": dict(name="Saint Paul Miki and Companions", dates="Died 1597 · Nagasaki", match=["miki"],
  history="Twenty-six Catholics crucified on a hill outside Nagasaki on 5 February 1597, under the regent Toyotomi Hideyoshi. They included three Japanese Jesuits, six Franciscan friars from Spain, Mexico and India, and seventeen Japanese laymen, among them catechists, craftsmen and three boys. Paul Miki, a Japanese Jesuit and a gifted preacher, preached his last sermon from the cross and forgave his executioners.",
  life="Christianity had come to Japan with Saint Francis Xavier in 1549 and spread widely, but by the 1590s Hideyoshi had come to fear it. In December 1596 he ordered the arrest of friars and Christians in Kyoto and Osaka. Each had part of an ear cut off, and they were marched hundreds of miles through the winter to Nagasaki as a warning to others. Paul Miki, born around 1564 into a well-to-do family and educated by the Jesuits, was close to ordination. On the cross he told the crowd that he was Japanese and a Christian, and that he forgave the regent and all who had caused his death. The martyrs sang psalms and encouraged one another until they were killed with lances. Persecution followed for more than two centuries, yet when missionaries returned in the 1860s they found hidden Christians who had kept the faith in secret. The martyrs were canonised in 1862.",
  prayerTo="Saint Paul Miki and holy martyrs of Japan, you sang and forgave as you died on your crosses. Pray for the Church in Japan and for Christians who suffer for their faith today. Pray for me, that I may never be ashamed of Christ, and that I may forgive those who wrong me as freely as you forgave. Amen.",
  penance="Pray for persecuted Christians and give to a charity that supports them. Forgive one person today who has hurt you, naming them before God.",
  meditation="From the cross Paul Miki prayed for the men who had condemned him. Is there anyone you would find it impossible to pray for?",
  query="Twenty-six Martyrs of Japan painting"),
 "02-08": dict(name="Saint Josephine Bakhita", dates="c. 1869–1947 · Sudan and Italy", match=["bakhita"],
  history="Kidnapped as a child in Darfur and sold into slavery, she suffered years of cruelty before being brought to Italy, where she came to know Christ through the Canossian Sisters. Declared free by an Italian court, she was baptised, entered their order and spent some fifty years in quiet service. She is patron of Sudan and of the victims of human trafficking.",
  life="Born in Darfur around 1869, she was seized by slave traders at about the age of eight. The shock was such that she forgot her own name, and her captors called her Bakhita, which means fortunate. She was bought and sold several times and treated with great cruelty; one owner had her body cut with scores of patterns and rubbed with salt. Eventually an Italian consul bought her in Khartoum and took her to Italy, where she became nanny to a family near Venice. Left in the care of the Canossian Sisters, she came to know the God she had sensed as a child. When her mistress wished to take her back to Africa she refused, and in 1889 an Italian court ruled that she was free. She was baptised Josephine in 1890 and professed as a Canossian in 1896. At Schio she served as cook, sacristan and doorkeeper, much loved by all. She died in 1947 and was canonised in 2000.",
  prayerTo="Saint Josephine Bakhita, you were enslaved and wounded, and in Christ you found the Master who loved you and set you free. Pray for all who are trafficked, enslaved or exploited, and for those who work to free them. Pray for me, that I may know I am loved by God and forgive as you forgave. Amen.",
  penance="Today is the World Day of Prayer against Human Trafficking. Pray for its victims and give to a charity that helps them, or find out whether something you buy is made by forced labour.",
  meditation="Benedict XVI wrote that Bakhita came to know she was definitively loved, and that whatever happened, this Love was waiting for her. Do you believe that about yourself?",
  query="Josephine Bakhita photograph"),
 "02-09": dict(name="Saint Cyril of Alexandria", dates="c. 376–444 · Alexandria", match=["cyril"], efMatch="Cyril",
  history="Patriarch of Alexandria for over thirty years and one of the great theologians of the Incarnation. When Nestorius of Constantinople taught that Mary should not be called Mother of God, Cyril defended the truth that Christ is one divine Person, and presided at the Council of Ephesus in 431, which upheld the title Theotokos. Leo XIII named him a Doctor of the Church.",
  life="Cyril succeeded his uncle Theophilus as patriarch of Alexandria in 412. His early years were marked by fierce conflict in a turbulent city, and his methods were sometimes harsh; the Church honours him for his teaching and his holiness, not for every act of his life. From 428 he gave himself to the question raised by Nestorius. If Mary is only the mother of the man Jesus, Cyril argued, then the one who was born, suffered and died for us is not truly God, and we are not saved. He wrote to Nestorius, to Rome and to the emperor, and Pope Celestine entrusted him with the case. At Ephesus in 431 the council deposed Nestorius and proclaimed Mary Mother of God, to the joy of the people. In 433 Cyril reached agreement with the bishops of Antioch, who had opposed him. His commentaries on Scripture are still read.",
  prayerTo="Saint Cyril, you fought for the truth that the Child of Mary is God made man. Pray for theologians and teachers, that they may serve the faith humbly and well. Pray for me, that I may honour Mary as Mother of God, worship her Son in spirit and truth, and defend the faith with charity as well as zeal. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Hail Mary slowly ten times today, dwelling each time on the words Mother of God, and make amends for one hard word you have spoken to another.",
  meditation="Cyril saw that everything depends on who Christ is. If someone asked you today who Jesus is, what would you say?",
  query="Cyril of Alexandria icon"),
 "02-10": dict(name="Saint Scholastica", dates="c. 480–c. 543 · Nursia and Monte Cassino", match=["scholastica"],
  history="The sister, and by tradition the twin, of Saint Benedict, she consecrated herself to God from childhood and led a community of women near Monte Cassino. All we know of her comes from Saint Gregory the Great, who tells of her last meeting with her brother, when her prayer won a storm that kept him with her through the night. She is patron of Benedictine nuns.",
  life="Gregory tells that Scholastica and Benedict met once a year at a house near the monastery, since she could not enter it. At their last meeting they spent the day in praise of God and holy conversation. As night fell she begged him to stay and talk until morning about the joys of heaven. Benedict refused, since his rule forbade a monk to spend a night away. Scholastica laid her head on her hands in prayer, and at once a storm broke so violently that he could not leave. ‘What have you done?’ he asked. She answered that she had asked him and he would not listen, so she had asked God, and God had listened. They talked until dawn. Three days later Benedict saw her soul rise to heaven in the form of a dove, and he buried her in the tomb he had prepared for himself. Gregory says she could do more because she loved more.",
  prayerTo="Saint Scholastica, you loved God and your brother so much that heaven answered your prayer. Pray for brothers and sisters, for families divided by distance or quarrels, and for all Benedictine monks and nuns. Pray for me, that I may love more, pray with confidence and long for the joys of heaven. Amen.",
  penance="Telephone or write to a brother, sister or old friend you have not spoken to for some time, and pray for them by name tonight.",
  meditation="She could do more, Gregory said, because she loved more. Where would a little more love change what you are able to do?",
  query="Saint Benedict and Saint Scholastica painting Restout"),
 "02-11": dict(name="Our Lady of Lourdes", dates="Apparitions of 1858", match=["lourdes"],
  history="On 11 February 1858 a lady in white appeared to Bernadette Soubirous, a poor girl of fourteen, at the grotto of Massabielle near Lourdes in the Pyrenees. In eighteen appearances she asked for prayer and penance, and on 25 March named herself: ‘I am the Immaculate Conception.’ The Church approved the apparitions in 1862, and Lourdes became one of the great places of pilgrimage, especially for the sick.",
  life="Bernadette’s family were so poor that they lived in a former jail. She was gathering firewood with her sister and a friend when she saw the lady in a hollow of the rock. In the following weeks crowds came with her, though only she saw the lady. On 25 February, told to drink at the spring, she scraped at the muddy ground until water came; the spring has flowed ever since, and of the many cures reported there the Church has recognised a small number as miraculous after strict medical examination. Questioned again and again by clergy and officials, Bernadette never changed her account. She did not know what the words Immaculate Conception meant, but they convinced her parish priest. In 1862 the bishop of Tarbes declared that the faithful were justified in believing. Lourdes is approved private revelation: Catholics are not bound to believe it, but the Church finds in it nothing contrary to faith.",
  prayerTo="Our Lady of Lourdes, you came to a poor and sickly girl and called the world to prayer and penance. Pray for all the sick, for those who care for them, and for all who come to you on pilgrimage. Pray for me, that I may come to the spring of grace your Son has opened, and be healed in body and soul as He wills. Amen.",
  penance="Today is the World Day of the Sick. Visit or telephone someone who is ill, or offer one of your own pains for them, and pray a decade of the Rosary, as Bernadette did at the grotto.",
  meditation="The lady told Bernadette that she did not promise to make her happy in this world, but in the next. What are you asking of God that He may wish to give in another way?",
  query="Our Lady of Lourdes apparition Bernadette painting"),
 "02-14": dict(name="Saints Cyril and Methodius", dates="c. 826–869 and c. 815–885 · Thessalonica, Moravia and Rome", match=["methodius"],
  history="Two brothers from Thessalonica in Greece who carried the Gospel to the Slav peoples of central Europe. They devised an alphabet for the Slavonic language, translated the Scriptures and the liturgy into it, and won the Pope’s approval for worship in the people’s own tongue. In 1980 Saint John Paul II named them patrons of Europe alongside Saint Benedict.",
  life="Constantine, later called Cyril, was a brilliant scholar and teacher at Constantinople; his elder brother Methodius had been a governor before becoming a monk. In 863 Prince Rastislav of Moravia asked the emperor for teachers who could instruct his people in their own language, and the brothers were sent. Cyril created the Glagolitic alphabet, from which the later Cyrillic script, named in his honour, developed. Their use of Slavonic in the liturgy was attacked by Frankish clergy, so they went to Rome, where Pope Adrian II received them warmly and approved their books. Cyril became a monk there and died in 869; he is buried in the basilica of San Clemente. Methodius, consecrated archbishop, returned to the Slavs. He was imprisoned for over two years by bishops who opposed him, freed at the Pope’s command, and worked on until his death in 885. The Slav churches of East and West venerate them as their apostles.",
  prayerTo="Saints Cyril and Methodius, apostles of the Slavs, you gave the Gospel to a people in their own words and suffered for it with patience. Pray for Europe, that it may remember its Christian roots. Pray for the unity of East and West, for missionaries and translators, and for me, that I may speak of Christ in words others can understand. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for the Christians of Eastern Europe and for unity between Catholics and Orthodox. Give to a charity that translates or distributes the Scriptures.",
  meditation="The brothers believed every people should be able to praise God in its own tongue. How could you speak of your faith in words your neighbours would understand?",
  query="Saints Cyril and Methodius painting Matejko"),
 "02-17": dict(name="The Seven Holy Founders of the Servite Order", dates="Thirteenth century · Florence and Monte Senario", match=["servite"],
  history="Seven men of Florence, members of a confraternity devoted to Our Lady, who left their homes and businesses in 1233 to live together in poverty and prayer. They withdrew to Monte Senario outside the city and founded the Order of the Servants of Mary, the Servites, known for their devotion to the Seven Sorrows of Our Lady. They were canonised together in 1888.",
  life="Thirteenth-century Florence was rich, busy and torn by faction. The seven, most of them merchants and some married or widowed, met in a lay confraternity devoted to the Virgin. Servite tradition holds that on the feast of the Assumption in 1233 Our Lady inspired them to leave the world. Having provided for their families, they lived together in a poor house outside the walls, then moved to the solitude of Monte Senario, where they lived in great austerity. People came to them for guidance, and they began to accept disciples. Adopting the Rule of Saint Augustine, they became an order of friars, preaching and serving in the towns, and received the final approval of the Holy See in 1304. In religion they were named Bonfilius, Alexis, Manettus, Amideus, Hugh, Sostenes and Buonagiunta. Alexis Falconieri, the last of them, lived to a great age and died on this day in 1310.",
  prayerTo="Holy Founders, you left the riches of Florence to stand with Mary beneath the cross of her Son. Pray for all who serve her, for the Servite friars and sisters, and for me, that I may share her sorrows with love, leave behind whatever keeps me from God, and find true brotherhood in Christ. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Rosary of the Seven Sorrows, or simply one Hail Mary for each of Our Lady’s sorrows. Give up one comfort today as an offering to her.",
  meditation="Seven busy men left their counting houses to stand with Mary at the foot of the cross. What would you need to leave to stand there with her?",
  query="Seven Holy Founders Servite Order painting"),
 "02-22": dict(name="The Chair of Saint Peter", dates="Rome, kept since the fourth century", match=["chair"], efMatch="Chair",
  history="A feast not of a piece of furniture but of the mission Christ gave to Peter: ‘You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my Church.’ The chair, or cathedra, is the sign of a bishop’s authority to teach. The Church honours the ministry of Peter and his successors, the bishops of Rome, as the visible foundation of its unity in faith and charity.",
  life="The feast is among the oldest in the Roman calendar. A list of Roman feasts from the year 354 already marks 22 February as the day of Peter’s chair. It may have begun as a Christian answer to the pagan custom of feasting for dead relatives at this time of year. For centuries Rome kept two feasts, one on 18 January for Peter’s chair at Rome and one today for his chair at Antioch, where he was held to have first presided; in 1960 they were joined. In Saint Peter’s Basilica, Bernini’s great bronze throne, borne up by four Doctors of the Church, encloses an ancient wooden chair long venerated as Peter’s. Above it a window of golden alabaster shows the Holy Spirit as a dove. It is a day to pray for the Pope and for the Church of Rome, which, in the words of Saint Ignatius of Antioch, presides in love.",
  prayerTo="Saint Peter, prince of the apostles, the Lord made you the rock of His Church and prayed that your faith would not fail. Pray for our Holy Father the Pope, that he may strengthen his brothers in the faith. Pray for the unity of the Church, and for me, that after every failure I may turn back, as you did, and strengthen others. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for the Pope and his intentions, an Our Father, a Hail Mary and a Glory Be, and offer a small sacrifice for the unity of the Church.",
  meditation="Christ built His Church on a man who had denied Him three times. What does that tell you about what He can build on you?",
  query="Cathedra Petri Bernini"),
 "02-23": dict(name="Saint Polycarp", dates="c. 69–c. 155 · Smyrna", match=["polycarp"],
  history="Bishop of Smyrna, in what is now Turkey, and a disciple of the apostle John. He received a letter from Saint Ignatius of Antioch, who was on his way to martyrdom, and long afterwards was martyred himself, burned at the stake in old age. The account of his death, written by his own church, is the oldest record of a Christian martyrdom outside the New Testament.",
  life="Saint Irenaeus, who knew Polycarp as a boy, remembered him speaking of his conversations with John and others who had seen the Lord. As bishop he guarded the faith against those who twisted it, and his letter to the Philippians, full of the words of the apostles, survives. In old age he travelled to Rome to discuss the date of Easter with Pope Anicetus; they did not agree, but they parted in peace, and Anicetus invited him to celebrate the Eucharist. When persecution broke out in Smyrna, Polycarp was found at a farm outside the city. He ordered food for those who came to arrest him and asked for time to pray. In the stadium the proconsul urged him to curse Christ. He replied that he had served Christ for eighty-six years and Christ had done him no wrong: how could he blaspheme his King who had saved him? The account says the flames formed an arch around him, and he was killed with a dagger.",
  prayerTo="Saint Polycarp, you heard the apostles and kept their teaching whole to the end. Pray for bishops, for the elderly who keep the faith alive, and for the unity of Christians. Pray for me, that I may stay faithful to Christ all my days and, when my hour comes, offer my life to Him in thanksgiving. Amen.",
  penance="Polycarp fed the men who came to arrest him. Do a kindness today for someone who has treated you badly, and pray for elderly people who live alone.",
  meditation="Eighty-six years I have served Him, and He has done me no wrong. Look back over your own life today and count the ways Christ has been faithful to you.",
  query="Martyrdom of Saint Polycarp painting"),
}
